{
  "requirement": "Each language's fingerprint rules determine which attachments\ncontribute to the owning item's structural hash.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "`requirements.sdk.outlines.attachments.fingerprint` says each language's fingerprint rules decide which attachments contribute to the owning item's structural hash. The obligations are per-language ones: attachments the rules include must change the owner's hash, and attachments the rules exclude must not. The language chapters set the rules, and the tests cover them as follows.\n\n- **Rust:** outer doc comments and attributes attach to the item and are hashed. Ordinary comments are excluded.\n  - `src/outline/tests.rs#fn[hashes_skip_whitespace_and_plain_comments_but_not_doc_comments]` builds a function with a doc comment, an attribute and a plain `// {cite}` comment between them.\n  - Changing the plain comment's text (`a.b` to `c.d`), the layout and the position leaves the hash unchanged.\n  - Changing the doc text (`Doc.` to `Docs.`) or the attribute (`inline` to `cold`) changes the hash. Changing the body also changes it.\n  - A `mod` case shows that a nested edit changes the parent's structural hash but not its identity hash.\n  - The implementation `Builder::encode` has a `hashes_comment` hook, and the Rust profile sets it to `is_doc_comment`. The test results match that split.\n- **Python:** decorators are attachments, and ordinary comments are excluded.\n  - `src/outline/tests.rs#fn[python_decorators_attach_to_the_decorated_definition_for_hashing]` shows that changing a decorator (`@cache` to `@other`) changes the hash.\n  - Removing the decorator also changes it, and blank-line or indent changes do not.\n  - A method's decorator changes the class's structural hash but not its identity hash.\n- **Other languages:** `tests/outline_content.rs#fn[bundled_fingerprints_exclude_comments_and_preserve_word_boundaries]` covers Rust, Python, TypeScript, TSX, CSS, Markdown, TOML and YAML.\n  - In each language, edited comments plus whitespace reflow keep the fingerprint. Joining words changes it.\n  - Rust doc comments and TypeScript `/** */` comments are covered by the same table. The TypeScript case drops the doc comment entirely in the joined-words variant, and the first two variants differ only in doc text and spacing.\n  - Python docstrings still participate in the hash, so a changed docstring changes it.\n  - A Markdown inline HTML comment still participates as paragraph text.\n\nTogether these cover the mechanism and each language's include and exclude decisions. I found no plausible violating behavior that the tests would accept. For example, a language that dropped attributes, doc comments or decorators from its hash, or hashed ordinary comments, would fail an assertion. The bundled profiles define no other hashed attachment kinds. The per-language wording is checked by the language chapters' own `fingerprints` requirements, which these tests also mark. No `{fixme}` acknowledgments apply.",
  "references": "2272dba8cdd32c9845b99dc74f77952eb96d2f2046ea64ab9502ddab77190019",
